    h1 My House Flooded — What to Expect From Here
    p.disclaimer
      em This article is for general information only and isn't specific advice. Every water-damage situation is different — for your circumstances, seek professional guidance.
    p The water's stopped, the panic's easing, and now you're staring at a soaked home wondering what happens next. Recovering a flooded house is a process, not a single job — here's the realistic arc, so you know what's coming and where the real work is.
    h2 Start with the immediate stuff
    p If it's still an active emergency — water coming in, electrical risk, contaminated water — deal with that first: stay safe, stop the source if you can, and photograph everything before you move things. Our guide to #[a(href='/blog/emergency-water-damage') emergency water damage] covers the first-minutes steps. Once it's safe and the water's stopped, the recovery proper begins.
    h2 The assessment sets the scope
    p Before anything gets dried or thrown out, the damage needs a proper look: how far the water actually travelled (it spreads well beyond the visible wet patch), how contaminated it was, and how long it sat. Floodwater from outside is dirtier than a burst pipe, which changes what can be kept. This assessment decides the whole recovery — what's salvageable, what has to go, and how long drying will take.
    h2 Drying comes before repairs — and it takes days
    p This is the part people underestimate. You can't repair a flooded home until it's genuinely dry, and "dry" is measured, not guessed. Extraction pulls the standing water; then air movers and dehumidifiers run continuously to draw moisture out of the underlay, subfloor and wall cavities, with moisture meters confirming it's back to the normal dry standard. For a whole-home flood that's typically several days, sometimes longer — the equipment stays on until the readings are met, noisy as it is.
    h2 Then repairs, in the right order
    p Once it's confirmed dry, recovery moves to putting things back: replacing saturated underlay and carpet, patching gyprock, repainting, and dealing with anything the water ruined. Some things clean up and come good; porous items soaked in contaminated floodwater usually don't. Our guide to #[a(href='/blog/fixing-water-damage') fixing water damage] covers the repair stage in detail.
    h2 Keep the insurance side moving
    p Recovery usually runs alongside an insurance claim, so hold on to your photos of the damage and any drying records the restorer provides — good documentation makes the claim smoother. It's worth letting your insurer know early, rather than after everything's already dried out and cleared away.
    h2 How long until life's back to normal?
    p Honestly, it depends on the scale — a contained flood in one room is a different job to a whole house. Drying is days; repairs can run longer depending on what needs replacing and trades' availability. The single biggest thing that shortens it is starting fast: the sooner proper drying is underway, the less spreads and the less there is to rebuild.
    h2 Getting recovery started
    p If your house has flooded, the fastest way to limit the damage is to get proper drying going before it sets in. drySafe is fully self-serve — check availability and book online in a few minutes. For the full picture of how water damage works, see our guide to #[a(href='/blog/water-damage') water damage].
